-ea52f01a197f" :topic/math-academy-id 2839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157314 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157118 :question/id #uuid "f86e3e4f-53a7-5a77-a97a-e019ab8bc9aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7500"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06c1ce9a-bad7-50d6-b6c7-a1423ccce4bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050757}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354436}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "1fa58b97-b6b2-5edd-8734-614f560a7651" :step/math-academy-id 27844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158162}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157315 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157119 :question/id #uuid "e43bd196-6952-5cce-bc7b-7dccc06e3b57" :question/math-academy-id "e-7501"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88cf7c0e-8c1d-5a92-805f-0a81ee887546" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305020}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "22e52493-867b-5374-829e-6bc8df9e2158" :step/math-academy-id 27845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158163}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157120 :question/id #uuid "04a21022-edc8-54c0-b5c9-232e600ac014" :question/math-academy-id "e-7502"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cd0921-2ed0-5b05-985a-e1849e98da10" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305024}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable on a Bounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "7a7fc129-73fd-5136-a99a-5948c4181281" :step/math-academy-id 27846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157317 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157121 :question/id #uuid "69ee8489-db49-5121-9a0a-34da81a02c22" :question/math-academy-id "e-7503"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42f47d95-9f20-5488-98c4-cb80278a7cf3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305028}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "23f94e12-bb19-5644-a2f6-54b67243d945" :step/math-academy-id 27847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158492 :tutorial/content "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/c6/c64756e38cf4152fdbcd3c3cb3c99e6368b7ec2549f6ea96e6badedeb7607cd8.svg)\n\nIf a random variable ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) follows a geometric distribution with probability ![](images/f4/f43942df7283a860efbcb722d0d28b46eca1b4d2ffc58a91a1858e36b0947101.svg) then we write ![](images/d1/d196f25e129c7b7bb4bdfff7f0383f5fa79d352b82ca3a0d8634fe7a64e0dc8c.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is ![](images/f9/f98838fb4a565f05ea528dffcab8dad9e199e52bb3a2dfe071d6e11955e9ceb6.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be the number of flips needed in order to get heads. Then ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/21/21bb1ed33c23f67b65a0c2337d7a34c17cd3e4f663b0b95737fff31bbafdf055.svg)\n\nIn particular, the probability of needing ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) tosses until we get heads is \n\n![](images/3c/3c965ff9951df068207847eb1c5eb105d486195f44f79adb654e43c8e50690c2.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d8664a30-c69c-59c8-9e1f-c2e46333f9be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4527 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6ff378f4-047d-5e9f-ba7d-3a98770af4f5" :step/math-academy-id 27852 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158494 :tutorial/content "The shape of the graph of the geometric distribution depends on the value of $p.$ When $p$ is large, the probabilities are concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $p$ is smaller, the probabilities are more spread out.\n\n![](images/51/51c0438105701a9ef6a19c280dde7bdddf6ccb82bd1ab487d2fdaf902bcca534.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ed7d42f-4e9c-5a46-9270-1af8549e921d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4535 :tutorial/title "The Graph of the Geometric Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "b13c477a-3272-5b6f-bce5-91e1831d0674" :step/math-academy-id 27933 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158161}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Geometric Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157494}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877} :activity/id #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" :step/math-academy-id 41352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" :step/math-academy-id 41353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" :step/math-academy-id 41354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" :step/math-academy-id 41357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" :step/math-academy-id 41358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" :step/math-academy-id 41359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120052}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563} :activity/id #uuid "76251565-5039-56a6-8121-2814efe41dc1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685 :topic/id #uuid "5ee1f34c-598f-5608-8eb7-1e1ffa254cd0" :topic/math-academy-id 1794} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120996 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) that's defined parametrically by \n![](images/a7/a74636de2b4b91905353ed8fd16c8af010260757f9cc70df2a24c23aef2ca55e.svg)\n\nThe point ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) corresponding to ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) on the curve has coordinates ![](images/51/51c3c69238e5ffe9c22e9a9a50dd89f69899db2c0895c5f39e2d80db74c6b3db.svg) We know that a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) is given by ![](images/b9/b9445cca9d656f361fdd089d2a38028714c21f8c5379a43731706bea0be49611.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4f4dad09042b761fe950c694c8cf645501106cd1d1d9c0f7a78967b224951c.png)\n\nWe compute ![](images/8a/8a5ec088ee8b89b6df1427fe455f10b0fc27842bf9bc4379d6b112daf01ec056.svg) as follows:\n![](images/88/8875ee3591bf9c6ccf0b3df9ca261c38894a134bb8229253145d000e6f6213fd.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) into the above gives a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/ed/ed03dfb662276f1766638162385f0cbef419ba1dd3a0ad02c9ee8d8a5d245cc9.svg)\n\n![](images/39/39ccc671a0f1a1b7df98a151741b7e9e46c0ee9cd8bfd858abe9a9ab4372f3d2.svg)\n\nAs we'll see, it's more helpful to work with unit vectors. Notice that ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) is not a unit vector because\n\n![](images/ae/ae44ee346812a97fc1e8935852d8615a4185458e1445cb39523d3003e26971f8.svg)\n\nHowever, we can create a unit vector, which we'll call ![](images/43/4326a76eb09b3a7888d11df0bb211ef28a4ab22bfd49a19ddcb96c9f9c98067e.svg) by dividing ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) by its magnitude:\n\n![](images/06/06ecc34ba9087997ec134c79f93d27ea2a0a3895c790ced864111add41ddc311.svg)\n\nIn general, for a vector valued function ![](images/e5/e5ced9a46fee369462905aace2537d978fd1222337b518b7fb49295793c28964.svg) the **unit tangent vector** ![](images/6f/6fd8da4e323d14ce0567026cff08b94311018226dcbd13f96777b640bb281fe9.svg) to the curve traced by ![](images/a0/a06debad6e153cb789c38ba57ea6e2ac7be55483b45b53a576dbbfa19715f637.svg) can be computed using the following formula:\n![](images/eb/eb59ef8c4faab00d31288eb311ed07c7787758c71d1e959ae7eabf198d7019a2.svg)\n\n**Note:** When we take a vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and create a unit vector that's parallel to it, we say that we have **normalized** the vector ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de55871f-089d-5982-98d6-3ea78f7e3b5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a2275fb-e73e-5073-a025-2bc601b975e5" :step/math-academy-id 10219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119553 :question/id #uuid "c44c0973-5253-50c5-98b1-15938b79494e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3054"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad